    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Computational thinking (CT) has become the cornerstone of many Career and Technical Education pathways, including computer science (CS) and engineering. Early CT is essential for future success in CS and engineering, like early natural language development is essential to speaking and learning. This study investigates how Universal Design for Learning (UDL) strategies are used to teach CT and how they relate to improving CT outcomes. We posit that UDL strategies can help educators teach CT in upper elementary school classrooms. Student pre- and post-CT data were gathered from 95 students in five upper elementary classrooms through a Bebras CT test. Data were analyzed with a paired t-test to determine statistical significance in the change of mean for students’ pre-and post-CT scores (Jacob et al., 2019). These scores were juxtaposed with coded and quantified UDL strategies, looking for trends in UDL use and changes in mean CT scores. All of the teachers observed used UDL strategies. The implementation of UDL strategies varied significantly from teacher to teacher. The teacher who used the most UDL strategies also had the greatest change in mean student CT scores from pre- post.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
